// Base URL for GitHub Pages
const baseUrl = '/serra2';

// Page metadata
const pageTitle = "The four artists of the regular editions - Serra 2.0";
const pageDescription = "Understanding the foundational principle behind Serra Angel and the Serra 2.0 project";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="description" content={pageDescription}>
  <title>{pageTitle}</title>
</head>

<body>
  <!-- Import Navigation Component -->
  <Navigation currentPage="about" />

  <!-- Page Header -->
  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>The four artists of the regular editions</h1>
    <p>The Foundational Principle Behind Serra Angel</p>
  </header>

  <!-- Main Content -->
  <main class="container">
    <article class="about-article">
      
      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          And Richard Garfield created Serra! Serra is the Latin word for talons, like those of an eagle, but also for a saw. 
          He wanted the angels of Magic: The Gathering to be representations of strength and power, wielding “serrated” swords. 
          Douglas Schuler, the first artist to depict Serra Angel, was not aware of this etymological reference, but he clearly understood the solemn power Richard Garfield was seeking. 
          His illustration draws from medieval and religious iconography typical of 1990s fantasy art. 
          It immediately establishes the Angel as a figure of righteousness, vigilance, and moral stability.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          According to cardmarket, there are 59 versions of Serra Angel, but only three other artists have worked on regular-edition printings: 
          <b>Mark Zug</b> (2001-2004), <b>Greg Staples</b> (2005-2014 then 2025 for Fondations) and <b>Donato Giancola</b> (2018-2023).
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="article-image1">
        <img src=`${baseUrl}/articles/the-four-artists-of-the-regular-editions.jpg` alt="Three images of the Angel throughout history">
      </div>

      <section class="article-section">
        <p>
          The depiction of Serra Angel over nearly thirty years clearly reflects the graphic evolution of Magic cards. 
          <b>Douglas Schuler</b> defines a luminous figure, dominated by whites and golds, which becomes the very image of white. 
          This is a simple, traditional treatment—what might be called “old school,” like the cards from the earliest sets. 
          <b>Mark Zug</b> retains the bright, celestial palette while rendering the Angel more realistically, moving away from a purely divine character. 
          Greg Staples significantly reduces the scale of the Angel in order to heighten the drama of this warrior angel. 
          The sky shifts to a black-and-white palette, featuring strong contrasts and striking light. 
          <b>Donato Giancola</b> preserves the proportions established by Greg Staples but places the Angel within a more dynamic scene, viewed from above, against a colorful background of “war colors.” 
          A city appears below, as if the Angel were falling, sword pointed downward—an image that echoes the card's diminished place in competitive Magic.
        </p>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>

  <!-- Footer -->
  <footer>
    <p>&copy; 2026 Serra 2.0 Project. All rights reserved.</p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>